# The comparator CHALLENGE — the kept customer surface, frozen as statements

[AGENT lane gate/comparator-1023] 2026-10-23, release-readiness row 43 ([USER]-ruled 2026-10-21
and 2026-10-23, paraphrased: use `lake comparator` where appropriate; with the relation retired,
the appropriate challenge is the statement set GoLean keeps on main under stable names for the
raft-proofs repository — `Prefix`/`Finish` and their laws, the equation library,
`enterFrame_declared`, the `Choices.consumeAt*` laws, the G11 family, the output-fold bridge and
what `Tests/GoCoreContract.lean` §D pins; a statement drift in that set must become a mismatch
the judge names, not a text diff). Since 2026-10-25 (lane `gate/contract-pins-1025`, [USER]-ruled 2026-10-25, backlog row 48's
follow-up) the set also carries G16's thirteen table-monotonicity statements (`TableMonotonicity.lean`,
family `g16` of scripts/contract-names.lean), moved here from `Tests/GoCoreContract.lean`'s former
§E Provisional once the per-key relation of train 17 was ruled final, plus `methodSetCoverage?_mono` at
the fix round the same day (the coordinator's decision: a first-match lookup of the same kind, named in
the customer's ask): 366 statements in all. The equation family is EVERY public theorem of
`Equations.lean` (300 since lane `core/g16-raise-mark-1023`; 234 of them `@[stepFn_eqns]` simp-set members — the customer's rewriting
library is the module, and its `signalStep_*` rows, `runProgramSetup_noInit`, the
`allocDecls_*`/`bindParams_*`/`setup_*` facts and the rest are plain theorems there): widened
from the simp set's membership at the fix round of 2026-10-23 (review F1), the owner's
confirmation asked at the merge ask.

## What this file is

The CHALLENGE half of `comparator.json` (repository root, `lake comparator`'s default path).
Every kept theorem `GoLean.<rest>` is restated here as `theorem GoLean.Contract.<rest> : <its
statement, spelled out> := sorry` — the statement FROZEN as text, the proof a hole. The SOLUTION
half is `GoLean/Contract/Solution.lean`, generated by scripts/gen-contract from the built
environment: `theorem GoLean.Contract.<rest> : stmt_of% GoLean.<rest> := GoLean.<rest>`, the kept
theorem's own type under the same name (GoLean/Contract/StmtOf.lean). `lake comparator` builds
and exports both modules and judges, per name in `theorem_names`: the two types are
SYNTACTICALLY equal (`Lake/Check/Compare.lean`, `compareAt`: the `ConstantVal`s must be `==`,
which is `Expr.eqv` — alpha-equivalence, so binder NAMES and binder ANNOTATIONS (explicit,
implicit, instance-implicit) are ignored, nothing else; today the challenge differs from the
kept types in binder names only, in 203 of the 349 at the freeze, and in no annotation); every definition the
statements reach is identical in both exports (the worklist compares each non-target constant
whole, value included); the solution's proofs rest on `propext`/`Quot.sound`/`Classical.choice`
alone (`permitted_axioms`; the challenge's `sorry` is never examined — `Lake/Check/Axioms.lean`
walks the solution only); and Lean's kernel (`leanchecker`) replays the SOLUTION export. The
judge of a drift is thus Lake's comparison of the two exports, not the kernel; the kernel's
part is the acceptance of the solution. Exit 0 accepts; exit 1 names the first mismatching
constant.

## Design (each choice with its reason)

* **Alias names, not the kept names.** The comparator matches `theorem_names` by FULL name in
  both exports, and a module cannot redeclare a name it imports. Some kept theorems live in the
  modules that define the vocabulary (`enterFrame_declared` in Machine.lean, the
  `Choices.consumeAt*` laws in State.lean/Machine.lean, `execProgLoopOut_snd` in Multi.lean), so
  a challenge under the kept names could not import the vocabulary. The alias of `GoLean.<rest>`
  is `GoLean.Contract.<rest>`: namespace-preserving, collision-free, mechanical (a consumer
  strips `Contract.`). The solution side is a thin generated module of aliases; no kept
  statement or proof is touched.
* **Statements spelled out; `*_stmt` constants never named.** The `Prefix`/`Finish` laws are
  `theorem <name> : <name>_stmt` over `def <name>_stmt : Prop` (ExecutionStatement.lean). A
  challenge stating `<name>_stmt` would compare one definition with itself — circular — so this
  file spells the proposition out and the solution's `stmt_of%` unfolds the `_stmt` head.
  scripts/gen-contract refuses a `*_stmt`, `stmt_of%`, `type_of%` or `Contract.Solution`
  spelling in this file's code.
* **Holes are `sorry`, the warning off PER DECLARATION.** Every entry is `set_option warn.sorry
  false in` (toolchain ≥ 4.35) ahead of its docstring and theorem — the option scoped to the one
  declaration whose hole it silences, never file-wide (lane `gate/contract-pins-1025`, 2026-10-25;
  the box rule for a warning that cannot be removed for a project-level reason: suppressed as
  narrowly as possible, the reason beside it, listed in the working-practices doc — CLAUDE.md
  «Speedbumps report» names this as the charter's one allowed warning suppression). The reason:
  a hole IS the challenge's form — the statement frozen, the proof the solution's — so the
  warning would name a defect that is the design; `sorryAx` is the universally recognised hole,
  and the comparator never trusts the challenge's proofs. scripts/gen-contract checks the form on
  the comment-stripped code: no file-wide option, one `set_option … in` per theorem, each followed
  by its `theorem GoLean.Contract.…`. This keeps the gate's warning-free build honest without a
  regex exemption. So this module is NOT a
  default target — `lake check` would refuse `sorryAx`, and its names would clash with the
  Solution's in one environment — but the non-default library `ContractChallenge`, built by
  name in the gate's `lake build` (so a renamed definition breaks it loudly there) and imported
  by nothing; scripts/ci's target-split check reads `challenge_module` from comparator.json and
  requires exactly that.
* **Imports: the vocabulary.** The executable core (`State`, `Machine`, `Multi`) and the
  definitions the statements name (`Prefix`/`Finish`/`FinishOutcome`/`replays`/`ZeroCost`/
  `Blocked` in ExecutionStatement.lean; `transferable`/`seqOpCount` in PoolFacts.lean;
  `seqOut`/`outFold` in PoolProjection.lean; `initPrintRefusal?` in Machine.lean;
  `ProgramCtx.SubTables` and the lookups it governs in TableMonotonicity.lean, since 2026-10-25).
  InitDriverFacts.lean is imported for one reason: two of its statements contain a `match`,
  which mints an auxiliary matcher constant per module (the cache behind
  `Lean.Meta.Match.mkMatcherAuxDefinition` is module-local), so the challenge names the kept
  theorem's own matcher (`runInitConfig_unfold.match_3`, printed with `pp.match false`);
  `enterFrame_declared` likewise names `enterFrame.plan.match_5` from Machine.lean. Importing a
  proof module is harmless under alias names; the circularity guard is the textual ban above.
* **Definitions are shared, not frozen — and the in-repo freeze is of the TEXT.** Every
  definition a statement reaches is compared for exact equality between the two exports —
  in-repo, main with main — and this file's text is re-elaborated against the LIVE definitions
  at every run. So an EDIT to a kept theorem's statement is caught (the frozen text no longer
  re-elaborates to the kept type), but a drift that enters through a definition's SIGNATURE —
  an inserted implicit or default argument (`optParam`/`autoParam`), an instance argument, an
  `abbrev` — moves the kept statement and the re-elaborated challenge alike, with no mismatch
  (review F3, 2026-10-23: `def f (x : Nat) (k : Nat := 1)` in a mini project, accepted). A
  change to `stepFn` or `Prefix` itself is seen by the differential corpus and by the equations'
  own proofs, not here. For a consumer holding a pinned export it is different:
  `lake comparator --challenge-from-export <the pin's export>` (`scripts/contract-export`)
  compares their definitions with ours as well, and catches both (docs/changelog/20d3946d-WINDOW.md).
* **The text is the delaborator's, verified.** scripts/contract-challenge-draft prints every
  kept statement from the environment and checks that the printed command re-elaborates, in
  this file's scope, to the identical `Expr` — the comparator's own test. 341 of the 349 at the freeze
  round-trip as printed (358 of the 366 since the G16 family joined, the same eight exceptions); the three with a `match`/`do` are printed with `pp.match false` and
  parenthesised (a `do` block would otherwise read the theorem's `:=` as a reassignment); four
  (`applyRhsOp_vals`, `applyStrictOp_toInterface_string`, `loadResults_nil`, `applyStmtOp_print`)
  in the source's dot-form `.ok` (the printed `Except.ok (…, [])` elaborates a tuple's
  `AccessTrace` component as `List MemEvent`: definitionally equal, syntactically not); one,
  `loadResults_cons`, by hand in the source's form (its printed list literal fixes the element
  type before `++` meets `tr : AccessTrace`). Each entry carries a note saying which.

## Updating this file

Deliberately, never by regeneration: a kept statement changes → `lake comparator` (the
`--diff`-tier speedbump scripts/check-comparator, and the claim-point run) names it → the change
is accepted here (scripts/contract-challenge-draft shows the new text) with a row in
docs/changelog/20d3946d-WINDOW.md naming the statement and why. A kept theorem added or removed
→ scripts/gen-contract regenerates comparator.json and the Solution, and `scripts/gen-contract
--check` names the statement this file lacks or no longer needs.

## Not expressible here, and what covers it

The `@[stepFn_eqns]` ATTRIBUTE itself (the simp set's membership): scripts/contract-names.lean
reads the set and prints its size as a COUNT line, requires every member to be a theorem of
`Equations.lean`, and gen-contract carries the count into its summary; a lemma that loses the
attribute stays in the family (the module's) but changes the count the records quote. `Decidable` instances and every other DEFINITION (`StateWf`'s instance, `replays`,
`Prefix`, `Finish`): shared, as above. The `example` controls of Prefix.lean,
ExecutionStatement.lean and §D: an `example` declares nothing an exporter can name; they stay
build-time controls. The no-unfold discipline of `Tests/EquationClient.lean`:
scripts/check-equations.
-/

